WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:05.474
Tyler: You know the application is slow.

2
00:00:05.474 --> 00:00:07.440
Tyler: The hard part is knowing where to look.

3
00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:15.479
Tyler: Today we'll follow this request with OBI, find the work behind the wait, and fix

4
00:00:15.479 --> 00:00:15.833
Tyler: it.

5
00:00:15.833 --> 00:00:20.088
Tyler: Then we'll come back to the customer's request and check the result.

6
00:00:20.088 --> 00:00:24.343
Tyler: We'll also stop repeating the instrumentation setup across the other services by

7
00:00:24.343 --> 00:00:27.180
Tyler: leveraging the OpenTelemetry Injector and its Kubernetes Operator.

8
00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:29.129
Nikola: I'm Nikola.

9
00:00:29.129 --> 00:00:30.700
Nikola: Tyler gets the first service.

10
00:00:30.700 --> 00:00:32.900
Nikola: I'll keep an eye on the rest.

11
00:00:33.220 --> 00:00:33.865
Tyler: I'm Tyler.

12
00:00:33.865 --> 00:00:36.766
Tyler: That sounds suspiciously like you've given me the homework.

13
00:00:36.766 --> 00:00:38.700
Tyler: Let's get straight to the system.

14
00:00:40.010 --> 00:00:43.483
Tyler: Here is our checkout application: eighteen services, with PostgreSQL and Redis.

15
00:00:43.483 --> 00:00:47.271
Tyler: The Gateway hands the request to Checkout, which calls Inventory and four

16
00:00:47.271 --> 00:00:47.903
Tyler: commerce branches.

17
00:00:47.903 --> 00:00:52.954
Tyler: The map shows what can talk to what; the duration tells us the whole request is

18
00:00:52.954 --> 00:00:53.270
Tyler: slow.

19
00:00:53.590 --> 00:00:56.770
Nikola: Every team can have a different definition of healthy.

20
00:00:56.770 --> 00:00:59.950
Nikola: Following the request gives us one shared starting point.

21
00:01:01.250 --> 00:01:03.635
Tyler: First, OpenTelemetry eBPF Instrumentation, or OBI.

22
00:01:03.635 --> 00:01:07.610
Tyler: We deploy it and let it discover the running applications.

23
00:01:07.930 --> 00:01:12.615
Nikola: That gives us service and dependency observations without setting up a language

24
00:01:12.615 --> 00:01:14.176
Nikola: SDK in each application.

25
00:01:14.176 --> 00:01:17.690
Nikola: We can start investigating while the applications keep running.

26
00:01:18.010 --> 00:01:19.030
Tyler: OBI is ready.

27
00:01:19.030 --> 00:01:20.730
Tyler: Let's open a checkout request.

28
00:01:25.290 --> 00:01:27.316
Nikola: The useful result is a connected trace.

29
00:01:27.316 --> 00:01:30.210
Nikola: This is the request behind the duration we started with.

30
00:01:34.530 --> 00:01:38.132
Tyler: Now we can see service calls, database work, and how their timings fit together.

31
00:01:38.132 --> 00:01:40.448
Tyler: But a connected trace is not a faster checkout.

32
00:01:40.448 --> 00:01:41.734
Tyler: The customer is still waiting.

33
00:01:41.734 --> 00:01:44.050
Tyler: We need to find the part we can change.

34
00:01:45.370 --> 00:01:48.324
Tyler: Watch the plain topology turn into an observed request path.

35
00:01:48.324 --> 00:01:52.459
Tyler: We can now ask which calls happened, which services handled them, and where the

36
00:01:52.459 --> 00:01:53.050
Tyler: time went.

37
00:01:53.370 --> 00:01:57.057
Nikola: Here we're following the Cart branch through Catalog, Recommendations, and

38
00:01:57.057 --> 00:01:57.426
Nikola: Promotions.

39
00:01:57.426 --> 00:02:01.850
Nikola: This is one branch of the same request moving through the system.

40
00:02:03.170 --> 00:02:04.480
Tyler: Start with the waterfall.

41
00:02:04.480 --> 00:02:09.391
Tyler: The Gateway span is long, but almost all of that time is spent waiting for

42
00:02:09.391 --> 00:02:09.718
Tyler: Checkout.

43
00:02:09.718 --> 00:02:12.010
Tyler: Checkout's total also contains its downstream calls.

44
00:02:12.330 --> 00:02:16.570
Nikola: So choosing the longest bar would send us back to the beginning.

45
00:02:16.570 --> 00:02:20.810
Nikola: Look instead at the gap before Checkout makes its first downstream call.

46
00:02:25.130 --> 00:02:27.891
Tyler: That interval is about a hundred and fifty milliseconds.

47
00:02:27.891 --> 00:02:31.266
Tyler: The downstream services haven't started yet, so they can't explain it.

48
00:02:31.266 --> 00:02:34.641
Tyler: Nearly half of this request is spent before that first call.

49
00:02:34.641 --> 00:02:38.323
Tyler: That is our first useful discovery: look inside Checkout, not at its

50
00:02:38.323 --> 00:02:38.630
Tyler: dependencies.

51
00:02:41.950 --> 00:02:45.970
Nikola: Now we have a specific question for language-level detail: what is Checkout

52
00:02:45.970 --> 00:02:47.310
Nikola: doing during that wait?

53
00:02:48.610 --> 00:02:50.072
Tyler: We have source access here.

54
00:02:50.072 --> 00:02:52.411
Tyler: The pricing calculation happens before the Inventory call.

55
00:02:52.411 --> 00:02:56.798
Tyler: We'll use the Java agent to time that method and expose the library work around

56
00:02:56.798 --> 00:02:57.090
Tyler: it.

57
00:02:57.410 --> 00:02:59.462
Nikola: This is OpenTelemetry's zero-code instrumentation.

58
00:02:59.462 --> 00:03:04.797
Nikola: The agent supplies the instrumentation; we still need to make it available, add

59
00:03:04.797 --> 00:03:08.490
Nikola: the startup setting, and configure where its data goes.

60
00:03:11.810 --> 00:03:13.206
Tyler: Here's the deployment change.

61
00:03:13.206 --> 00:03:15.300
Tyler: The application image stays the same.

62
00:03:15.300 --> 00:03:19.836
Tyler: We provide the agent, set the service identity and exporter, and enable the

63
00:03:19.836 --> 00:03:20.534
Tyler: pricing-method span.

64
00:03:20.534 --> 00:03:21.930
Tyler: Then we roll Checkout.

65
00:03:22.250 --> 00:03:23.628
Nikola: Zero code is useful.

66
00:03:23.628 --> 00:03:26.041
Nikola: It hasn't made the release calendar disappear.

67
00:03:26.041 --> 00:03:30.865
Nikola: Someone still needs the runtime knowledge, the settings, and a way to deploy the

68
00:03:30.865 --> 00:03:31.210
Nikola: change.

69
00:03:41.490 --> 00:03:44.349
Tyler: Here's a fresh request with the Java agent running.

70
00:03:44.349 --> 00:03:46.890
Tyler: The missing interval now has a name: PricingEngine.calculate.

71
00:03:51.210 --> 00:03:54.580
Nikola: There it is: about a hundred and fifty milliseconds.

72
00:03:54.580 --> 00:03:59.821
Nikola: Below it, Hibernate and JDBC show the product lookup, the order insert, and the

73
00:03:59.821 --> 00:04:00.570
Nikola: transaction commit.

74
00:04:03.890 --> 00:04:05.820
Tyler: OBI got us to the right service.

75
00:04:05.820 --> 00:04:08.026
Tyler: The language agent named the work inside it.

76
00:04:08.026 --> 00:04:10.231
Tyler: But naming the wait does not remove it.

77
00:04:10.231 --> 00:04:11.610
Tyler: Let's open that pricing method.

78
00:04:12.930 --> 00:04:15.144
Tyler: There is our hundred and fifty milliseconds.

79
00:04:15.144 --> 00:04:18.306
Tyler: This demo deliberately puts a wait inside the pricing method.

80
00:04:18.306 --> 00:04:22.734
Tyler: It is local work, before the first downstream call, exactly where the trace sent

81
00:04:22.734 --> 00:04:23.050
Tyler: us.

82
00:04:25.870 --> 00:04:30.184
Nikola: So the fix is small: remove that wait and keep the same price.

83
00:04:30.184 --> 00:04:32.839
Nikola: The instrumentation did not make the application faster.

84
00:04:32.839 --> 00:04:34.830
Nikola: It showed us where to act.

85
00:04:35.150 --> 00:04:37.021
Tyler: Here we deploy the fixed application image.

86
00:04:37.021 --> 00:04:41.030
Tyler: The Java agent, its settings, and the rest of the request path stay the same.

87
00:04:56.470 --> 00:04:57.910
Nikola: The deployment is ready.

88
00:04:57.910 --> 00:05:00.790
Nikola: A completed rollout is not the customer result.

89
00:05:00.790 --> 00:05:02.590
Nikola: Let's send the request again.

90
00:05:05.240 --> 00:05:06.604
Tyler: Back to the customer request.

91
00:05:06.604 --> 00:05:10.424
Tyler: In a fresh matched run, the median goes from about three hundred and sixty

92
00:05:10.424 --> 00:05:12.061
Tyler: milliseconds to two hundred and twenty-six.

93
00:05:12.061 --> 00:05:15.880
Tyler: The pricing method goes from a hundred and fifty milliseconds to less than one.

94
00:05:18.700 --> 00:05:19.399
Nikola: Same price.

95
00:05:19.399 --> 00:05:23.944
Nikola: An order still gets created, and the request still visits all eighteen services.

96
00:05:23.944 --> 00:05:28.140
Nikola: We removed the deliberate wait, not the work needed to finish checkout.

97
00:05:28.460 --> 00:05:32.663
Tyler: These are medians of thirty warmed requests on each version, with the same

98
00:05:32.663 --> 00:05:32.987
Tyler: instrumentation.

99
00:05:32.987 --> 00:05:36.220
Tyler: It is a measured demo result, not a production benchmark.

100
00:05:36.540 --> 00:05:38.514
Nikola: Now that is a useful trace.

101
00:05:38.514 --> 00:05:40.817
Nikola: It helped us change the customer experience.

102
00:05:40.817 --> 00:05:45.424
Nikola: The next question is how to make this kind of investigation available to the

103
00:05:45.424 --> 00:05:46.740
Nikola: rest of the teams.

104
00:05:48.050 --> 00:05:49.447
Nikola: We've fixed this checkout.

105
00:05:49.447 --> 00:05:53.290
Nikola: But getting to that answer needed runtime knowledge, configuration, access, and

106
00:05:53.290 --> 00:05:53.989
Nikola: a deployment.

107
00:05:53.989 --> 00:05:58.530
Nikola: How do the other teams get this detail without repeating all that setup?

108
00:05:58.850 --> 00:06:02.658
Tyler: In this demo, thirteen more services use runtimes in the Injector payload.

109
00:06:02.658 --> 00:06:04.879
Tyler: The Go services keep their OBI coverage.

110
00:06:04.879 --> 00:06:08.370
Tyler: Our next step is to share the setup across those thirteen.

111
00:06:08.690 --> 00:06:11.050
Nikola: And share the routine work around it.

112
00:06:11.050 --> 00:06:15.770
Nikola: Not every team has our access, our runtime knowledge, or an empty release queue.

113
00:06:17.090 --> 00:06:20.302
Tyler: The upstream OpenTelemetry Operator can supply the agent.

114
00:06:20.302 --> 00:06:24.318
Tyler: You opt in using language-specific annotations, on workloads or namespaces.

115
00:06:24.318 --> 00:06:27.530
Tyler: That already removes much of the agent setup.

116
00:06:27.850 --> 00:06:32.116
Nikola: For this mixed-language application, we want one shared choice: instrument the

117
00:06:32.116 --> 00:06:32.504
Nikola: namespace.

118
00:06:32.504 --> 00:06:36.770
Nikola: Then let the system choose the agents and manage the rollout.

119
00:06:38.090 --> 00:06:41.703
Tyler: This demo uses the custom namespace-driven controller and the OpenTelemetry

120
00:06:41.703 --> 00:06:42.065
Tyler: Injector.

121
00:06:42.065 --> 00:06:44.955
Tyler: The controller selects workloads and manages their rollout.

122
00:06:44.955 --> 00:06:48.930
Tyler: The Injector detects the supported runtime and loads its language agent.

123
00:06:49.250 --> 00:06:52.015
Nikola: Here we install the controller once.

124
00:06:52.015 --> 00:06:56.163
Nikola: The agent payload, exporter, and exclusions are shared settings.

125
00:06:56.163 --> 00:06:59.850
Nikola: Next comes the application choice: instrument this namespace.

126
00:07:01.170 --> 00:07:04.707
Nikola: Here is the whole selection rule: instrument demo-apps.

127
00:07:04.707 --> 00:07:06.917
Nikola: No list of service names.

128
00:07:06.917 --> 00:07:09.570
Nikola: No language-specific annotations on each application.

129
00:07:12.890 --> 00:07:17.050
Tyler: Apply that rule, and the controller rolls the selected workloads.

130
00:07:17.050 --> 00:07:19.130
Tyler: The Injector supplies their agents.

131
00:07:19.130 --> 00:07:23.290
Tyler: The shared rule replaces thirteen deployment edits and manual restarts.

132
00:07:37.880 --> 00:07:40.769
Nikola: Now all the supported services are lit up.

133
00:07:40.769 --> 00:07:44.742
Nikola: Checkout keeps its direct agent, thirteen more services gain language detail,

134
00:07:44.742 --> 00:07:47.270
Nikola: and the four Go services keep OBI.

135
00:07:47.270 --> 00:07:50.520
Nikola: One namespace choice carries the setup across the application.

136
00:07:51.850 --> 00:07:56.245
Tyler: Back in the application view, we're looking at a new request through the

137
00:07:56.245 --> 00:07:56.921
Tyler: instrumented fleet.

138
00:07:56.921 --> 00:08:01.316
Tyler: We still have the system view, and now we can open language-level observations

139
00:08:01.316 --> 00:08:02.330
Tyler: in more services.

140
00:08:02.650 --> 00:08:03.796
Nikola: Let's follow Catalog.

141
00:08:03.796 --> 00:08:07.617
Nikola: These are the Java agent's database spans from that service.

142
00:08:07.617 --> 00:08:12.966
Nikola: The same enrollment also covers the Node, Python, Ruby, and dot net services in

143
00:08:12.966 --> 00:08:13.730
Nikola: this application.

144
00:08:14.050 --> 00:08:18.137
Tyler: The namespace rule got us here without a separate instrumentation change from

145
00:08:18.137 --> 00:08:19.159
Tyler: each application team.

146
00:08:19.159 --> 00:08:20.522
Tyler: Checkout keeps its fix.

147
00:08:20.522 --> 00:08:24.949
Tyler: Now the other teams have the language detail ready for their next investigation

148
00:08:24.949 --> 00:08:25.290
Tyler: too.

149
00:08:26.610 --> 00:08:28.744
Tyler: Think about the direct setup we watched.

150
00:08:28.744 --> 00:08:31.792
Tyler: We knew the runtime, had access, and controlled the release.

151
00:08:31.792 --> 00:08:35.450
Tyler: How much happens before someone in a real organization can even begin?

152
00:08:35.770 --> 00:08:37.010
Nikola: Find an owner.

153
00:08:37.010 --> 00:08:38.250
Nikola: Explain the request.

154
00:08:38.250 --> 00:08:42.383
Nikola: A ticket, a meeting, access, review, and a release slot.

155
00:08:42.383 --> 00:08:45.690
Nikola: Then somebody switches away from their feature work.

156
00:08:46.010 --> 00:08:49.802
Tyler: One namespace choice can remove repeated enrollment changes and keep shared

157
00:08:49.802 --> 00:08:50.492
Tyler: settings consistent.

158
00:08:50.492 --> 00:08:53.250
Tyler: The saving is more than typing fewer commands.

159
00:08:53.570 --> 00:08:56.059
Nikola: It is fewer people arranging the same work.

160
00:08:56.059 --> 00:08:59.170
Nikola: Count their active effort separately from the time spent waiting.

161
00:09:00.490 --> 00:09:02.462
Tyler: And setup is only the first bill.

162
00:09:02.462 --> 00:09:03.589
Tyler: A new service arrives.

163
00:09:03.589 --> 00:09:04.998
Tyler: An agent needs an update.

164
00:09:04.998 --> 00:09:06.970
Tyler: A team needs to turn instrumentation off.

165
00:09:07.290 --> 00:09:10.553
Nikola: Compatible new services inherit the namespace choice.

166
00:09:10.553 --> 00:09:13.349
Nikola: Updates reuse the shared rollout path.

167
00:09:13.349 --> 00:09:17.544
Nikola: Configuration, documentation, and recovery instructions have one place to

168
00:09:17.544 --> 00:09:18.010
Nikola: improve.

169
00:09:18.330 --> 00:09:20.790
Tyler: That can reduce drift and repeated troubleshooting.

170
00:09:20.790 --> 00:09:23.250
Tyler: But the platform does not maintain itself.

171
00:09:23.570 --> 00:09:24.042
Nikola: Exactly.

172
00:09:24.042 --> 00:09:27.346
Nikola: Compatibility, rollout, and recovery still need people.

173
00:09:27.346 --> 00:09:30.650
Nikola: Their time goes into the calculation too.

174
00:09:31.970 --> 00:09:34.850
Tyler: Let us compare the actual work in each approach.

175
00:09:34.850 --> 00:09:38.690
Tyler: This is a hypothetical year for our eighteen-service system, at a hundred

176
00:09:38.690 --> 00:09:39.650
Tyler: dollars per person-hour.

177
00:09:39.970 --> 00:09:42.787
Nikola: Thirteen compatible services remain after our pilot.

178
00:09:42.787 --> 00:09:47.213
Nikola: Initial setup takes thirty-two and a half person-hours across those teams.

179
00:09:47.213 --> 00:09:52.846
Nikola: With the shared tools, we allow two hours for deployment and policy, plus half

180
00:09:52.846 --> 00:09:54.456
Nikola: an hour per service.

181
00:09:54.456 --> 00:09:56.870
Nikola: Eight and a half hours altogether.

182
00:09:57.190 --> 00:09:59.898
Tyler: Upkeep is where centralization keeps paying back.

183
00:09:59.898 --> 00:10:03.766
Tyler: Four updates take thirty-nine hours when repeated across those services.

184
00:10:03.766 --> 00:10:06.087
Tyler: Four shared updates take four hours.

185
00:10:06.087 --> 00:10:10.342
Tyler: Allow another two hours for service exceptions: six hours instead of

186
00:10:10.342 --> 00:10:10.729
Tyler: thirty-nine.

187
00:10:10.729 --> 00:10:13.050
Tyler: That saves thirty-three hours every year.

188
00:10:13.370 --> 00:10:17.445
Nikola: Then twelve investigations where OBI adds missing visibility: eighteen

189
00:10:17.445 --> 00:10:18.804
Nikola: person-hours becomes six.

190
00:10:18.804 --> 00:10:20.615
Nikola: Twelve more hours saved.

191
00:10:20.615 --> 00:10:22.879
Nikola: Already have equally useful traces?

192
00:10:22.879 --> 00:10:24.690
Nikola: That part is zero.

193
00:10:25.010 --> 00:10:30.604
Tyler: Add the three rows: eighty-nine and a half hours before, twenty and a half with

194
00:10:30.604 --> 00:10:31.350
Tyler: the tools.

195
00:10:31.350 --> 00:10:34.706
Tyler: Sixty-nine hours saved, worth sixty-nine hundred dollars of engineering

196
00:10:34.706 --> 00:10:35.079
Tyler: capacity.

197
00:10:35.079 --> 00:10:37.690
Tyler: Central setup and upkeep are already included.

198
00:10:38.010 --> 00:10:42.410
Nikola: Every time estimate is an assumption to replace with your own.

199
00:10:42.410 --> 00:10:46.010
Nikola: Prototype development, extra telemetry, and support need separate estimates.

200
00:10:47.330 --> 00:10:51.658
Tyler: Keep the same runtime mix, and allow more rollout groups and service exceptions

201
00:10:51.658 --> 00:10:52.990
Tyler: as the fleet grows.

202
00:10:52.990 --> 00:10:56.652
Tyler: At a hundred services, the first-year value is about thirty-eight thousand

203
00:10:56.652 --> 00:10:56.985
Tyler: dollars.

204
00:10:56.985 --> 00:11:00.648
Tyler: At five hundred services, about a hundred and ninety-three thousand dollars.

205
00:11:00.648 --> 00:11:04.310
Tyler: At a thousand services, about three hundred and eighty-six thousand dollars.

206
00:11:04.630 --> 00:11:09.182
Nikola: The following-year figures are lower because initial setup savings happen once.

207
00:11:09.182 --> 00:11:14.147
Nikola: Centralized upkeep keeps saving work: updates happen per rollout group, plus the

208
00:11:14.147 --> 00:11:16.630
Nikola: service exceptions we explicitly allow for.

209
00:11:16.950 --> 00:11:19.720
Tyler: These are replaceable assumptions, not average customer results.

210
00:11:19.720 --> 00:11:23.182
Tyler: If existing namespace templates already centralize your updates, compare against

211
00:11:23.182 --> 00:11:23.874
Tyler: that workflow.

212
00:11:23.874 --> 00:11:26.990
Tyler: There may be little extra work left to save.

213
00:11:27.310 --> 00:11:30.481
Nikola: And capacity is not automatically cash in the bank.

214
00:11:30.481 --> 00:11:34.710
Nikola: We have not priced outages, waiting time, or a smaller telemetry bill.

215
00:11:36.010 --> 00:11:38.832
Tyler: We started with a customer waiting for checkout.

216
00:11:38.832 --> 00:11:40.948
Tyler: OBI showed where the time went.

217
00:11:40.948 --> 00:11:43.417
Tyler: The Java agent named the pricing method.

218
00:11:43.417 --> 00:11:47.650
Tyler: We removed the deliberate wait and came back to measure the request.

219
00:11:47.970 --> 00:11:51.874
Nikola: Then one namespace choice carried language instrumentation across the supported

220
00:11:51.874 --> 00:11:52.264
Nikola: services.

221
00:11:52.264 --> 00:11:57.340
Nikola: The next team can start with useful detail instead of starting another setup

222
00:11:57.340 --> 00:11:57.730
Nikola: project.

223
00:11:58.050 --> 00:11:59.661
Tyler: The customer gets a faster checkout.

224
00:11:59.661 --> 00:12:02.884
Tyler: The teams get a shared way to investigate the next slow request.

225
00:12:02.884 --> 00:12:05.570
Tyler: That is what the traces and the automation were for.

226
00:12:05.890 --> 00:12:06.930
Nikola: Find the wait.

227
00:12:06.930 --> 00:12:07.970
Nikola: Fix the cause.

228
00:12:07.970 --> 00:12:09.703
Nikola: Make the next investigation easier.

229
00:12:09.703 --> 00:12:11.090
Nikola: Let's take your questions.
